R = {
    2553: {
        "a": "Reduce drag by streamlining the engine cowling at all airspeeds",
        "d": "Prevent carburettor icing by warming the air entering the intake",
        "e": "Cowl flaps are adjustable flaps at the rear of the engine cowling that let the pilot control how much cooling air flows through the engine compartment, keeping the cylinder head and oil temperatures within limits. They are normally open on the ground and in the climb, when airspeed is low and power high, and closed in the cruise and descent to reduce drag and avoid over-cooling. Baffles are fixed plates that simply direct the air around the cylinders.",
    },
    2554: {
        "a": "Advance automatically",
        "b": "Retard automatically",
        "c": "Vary with the throttle setting",
        "e": "Most light-aircraft piston engines have fixed ignition timing: once the engine is running and the impulse coupling has disengaged, each magneto fires at the same crank angle (typically 20 to 25 degrees before TDC) whatever the RPM. Higher RPM means the sparks come more often, not earlier. Unlike many car engines there is no automatic advance or retard; the only retard is the impulse coupling's, during starting.",
    },
    2556: {
        "b": "At TDC at the end of the compression stroke",
        "d": "Between 20 degrees and 25 degrees before TDC during the exhaust stroke",
        "e": "Ignition is timed to occur before TDC on the compression stroke, typically 20 to 25 degrees, because the mixture takes a short time to burn. Firing early lets the pressure peak just after TDC, as the piston begins the power stroke, which gives the most useful work. A spark at or after TDC would leave the piston running away from the burning mixture, wasting power and overheating the engine, and there is nothing to ignite on the exhaust stroke.",
    },
    2560: {
        "d": "The inlet valve closing before the piston reaches BDC",
        "e": "Valve overlap is the short period around TDC, at the end of the exhaust stroke and the start of induction, when both valves are open together: the inlet has opened early (valve lead) and the exhaust has not yet closed (valve lag). The outgoing exhaust gases help draw in the fresh charge, improving scavenging and filling at normal operating RPM. Inlet opening before TDC is valve lead and inlet closing after BDC is valve lag.",
    },
    2561: {
        "e": "The CHT probe, usually a thermocouple under a spark plug or in a threaded well, is fitted to the cylinder that flight tests show runs the hottest, often one with the least cooling airflow. If that cylinder is kept within limits, the cooler cylinders will be too. Engines with a multi-probe engine monitor show every cylinder, but a single CHT gauge reads only the hottest one - not the coolest, the exhaust or the oil.",
    },
    2562: {
        "a": "When the mixture is set to full rich",
        "b": "Only when the engine is cold",
        "c": "When fuel of a higher octane than required is used",
        "e": "Pre-ignition happens when something in the combustion chamber is hot enough to ignite the fresh charge before the spark: a glowing carbon deposit, an overheated spark-plug electrode or a red-hot exhaust valve. It is therefore linked to overheating, lean mixtures and carbon build-up, not to cold engines or rich mixtures, which run cooler. It causes rough running and power loss, and can let the engine run on after the ignition is switched off.",
    },
    2563: {
        "e": "The octane rating measures a fuel's resistance to detonation. With a lower grade than the engine needs, the end gas - the last of the charge ahead of the flame front - can explode spontaneously under the heat and pressure of combustion, which is detonation. It causes loss of power, overheating and, if prolonged, damage to pistons and valves. Pre-ignition is caused by hot spots rather than the fuel grade, and valve lead and lag are timing terms.",
    },
    2564: {
        "c": "Reduce the noise from the engine",
        "d": "Prevent oil leaking onto the exhaust",
        "e": "Baffles are fixed metal plates and seals around the cylinders that force the incoming cooling air to flow closely over and between the cylinder fins, instead of taking the easy path around them, so every cylinder is cooled evenly. They work with the pressure difference created by the cowling. Controlling how much air flows to keep the right temperature is the job of the cowl flaps; baffles only direct it.",
    },
    2726: {
        "e": "Engine power depends on the mass of air, and so of oxygen, drawn into the cylinders on each stroke. Cold air is denser than warm air, high pressure means denser air, and dry air is denser than humid air because water vapour is lighter than the nitrogen and oxygen it displaces. So cold, dry, high-pressure air is the densest and gives the most power - worth remembering on hot, humid days at high-elevation airfields such as those on the Highveld.",
    },
    2729: {
        "e": "Beyond best economy there is too little fuel for the air: the mixture burns slowly and unevenly, so the engine runs rough and loses power, and if leaning continues it will eventually stop. Because less fuel is being burnt, the exhaust gas and cylinder head temperatures generally fall on this lean side of peak EGT. Excessive leaning never increases RPM or makes the mixture richer, and it does not raise the manifold pressure.",
    },
    2730: {
        "e": "The ideal Otto cycle, on which the petrol engine is based, has four processes: adiabatic compression (the compression stroke), heat added at constant volume (combustion, assumed instantaneous at TDC), adiabatic expansion (the power stroke) and heat rejected at constant volume (the exhaust valve opening at BDC). The area enclosed by these two adiabatic and two constant-volume lines on the pressure-volume diagram is the useful work done per cycle.",
    },
    2741: {
        "e": "The wastegate controls a turbocharger by letting some exhaust gas bypass the turbine. If it sticks closed, all the exhaust drives the turbine and the compressor keeps delivering maximum boost, so in a descent, where the air is denser, the manifold pressure can rise above its limit (an overboost), risking detonation and engine damage. The pilot must watch the manifold pressure and reduce the throttle as needed; the turbine does not necessarily fail and power does not fall to idle.",
    },
    2742: {
        "e": "Power is the rate of doing work. For a rotating shaft the work done in each revolution depends on the torque, so power equals torque multiplied by angular speed (P = T × ω), which is why engine power rises with RPM for the same torque. Force multiplied by distance is work, not power; power is work divided by time, not multiplied by it.",
    },
    2744: {
        "e": "Fuel entering the carburettor first passes through a fine strainer at the fuel inlet, upstream of the float needle valve. It catches dirt before it can stop the needle valve seating, which would flood the carburettor, or block the small metering jets further downstream. A strainer placed after the needle valve would be too late to protect it.",
    },
    2747: {
        "e": "A carburettor meters fuel by the volume of air flowing through it, not its mass. As altitude increases the air becomes less dense, so each volume of air contains less oxygen, but the carburettor still delivers about the same amount of fuel. There is then too much fuel for the mass of air and the mixture becomes rich, which is why the pilot leans the mixture in the climb and at cruising altitude.",
    },
    2749: {
        "e": "With the magnetos properly earthed there is no spark when the ignition is switched off, so a hot engine that keeps running must be igniting its charge some other way: glowing carbon deposits or an overheated spark-plug electrode act as hot spots and fire the mixture, a form of pre-ignition. This is one reason engines are shut down with the mixture at idle cut-off, which starves them of fuel. An open condenser or a fouled plug reduces sparks rather than creating them.",
    },
    2752: {
        "e": "A short stroke means a shorter crank throw and less piston travel, so the cylinders, crankshaft and crankcase can be smaller and lighter, and the engine can run at the RPM a directly driven propeller needs without excessive piston speeds. It does not remove the need for piston cooling or lubrication, and it cannot guarantee lower fuel consumption.",
    },
    2755: {
        "e": "The crank assembly is the group of parts that turns the pistons' up-and-down movement into rotation: the pistons, the connecting rods that link them to the crankpins, and the crankshaft itself. The camshaft, valves, springs and pushrods make up the separate valve-operating mechanism, the crankcase is the housing, and the propeller is driven by the crankshaft rather than being part of the assembly.",
    },
    2756: {
        "e": "The pistons move up and down, and a connecting rod joins each piston to an offset crankpin on the crankshaft. As the piston pushes on the crankpin the crankshaft turns, converting reciprocating motion into rotary motion - much like a cyclist's legs turning the pedal cranks. The camshaft opens the valves, the piston (gudgeon) pin links piston and connecting rod, and a reduction gear, where fitted, lowers the propeller speed.",
    },
    2760: {
        "e": "A four-stroke cycle takes two crankshaft revolutions, and each cylinder fires only once per cycle, near the end of its compression stroke. The spark is timed before TDC so the mixture has time to burn and reach peak pressure just after TDC. So ignition in each cylinder occurs before TDC on every second revolution; firing on every revolution would put a spark into the exhaust stroke as well.",
    },
    2761: {
        "e": "Compression ratio compares the total volume of the cylinder with the piston at BDC (swept volume plus clearance volume) with the volume left when the piston is at TDC (the clearance or combustion-chamber volume). A typical light-aircraft engine has a ratio of about 7:1 to 8.5:1; a higher ratio gives more power and efficiency but needs a higher-octane fuel to avoid detonation. Bore divided by stroke is the bore-stroke ratio, a different quantity.",
    },
    2770: {
        "e": "Fuel stores chemical energy. Burning it in the cylinder releases that energy as heat, which raises the pressure of the gases; the expanding gases push the piston down, and the crankshaft turns that movement into mechanical energy that drives the propeller. So the engine converts chemical energy to heat and then to mechanical work. Electrical energy only appears if the engine drives an alternator.",
    },
    2772: {
        "e": "Wet, black, oily deposits in the firing end of a spark plug mean lubricating oil is reaching the combustion chamber and burning, typically past worn piston rings or valve guides, and they are most often found on the lower plugs. A rich mixture leaves dry, black, sooty carbon instead, while normal combustion leaves light grey or tan deposits.",
    },
}
